  "question": "1) Background / Context\n\nIn the modern IT landscape, server reliability is crucial for maintaining uninterrupted services. Servers may face issues such as full disks, process crashes, and memory shortages, necessitating continuous monitoring to ensure optimal performance. This project involves developing a server monitoring system that oversees the health of multiple servers by performing various checks like disk space usage and verifying the status of services such as Elasticsearch, Memcached, and MySQL.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of this system is to provide automated, real-time monitoring of server health with minimal maintenance requirements. It aims to promptly identify and alert administrators about potential issues before they impact server performance.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- System Administrators: Responsible for configuring and managing the monitoring checks and responding to alerts.\n- IT Managers: Interested in overall server performance and uptime.\n- Developers: May need insights for maintaining and updating server-dependent applications.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Monitoring Checks: Out-of-the-box checks for disk space, process availability (e.g., Elasticsearch, Memcached, MySQL), with the ability for users to add custom checks.\n- Alerting: Real-time notifications for any check failures or threshold breaches.\n- Server Management: Maintain and update server details such as name, SSH user, port, and IP.\n- Check Scheduling: Configurable scheduling of checks with options for enabling/disabling specific checks.\n- Reports & Logs: Provide logs and reports of check results, including last run status and output.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Capable of monitoring a large number of servers simultaneously.\n- Usability: Intuitive user interface for configuring and managing checks with minimal effort.\n- Performance: Ensure low-latency notification and minimal resource overhead on monitored servers.\n- Reliability: High availability of monitoring functions and fail-safe alerting mechanisms.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0076\nCREATE TABLE `servers` (\n  `server_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `server_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `ssh_user` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `ssh_port` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 22,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`server_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `checks` (\n  `check_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `check_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `check_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(500) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_custom` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `default_threshold` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`check_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `server_checks` (\n  `server_check_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `server_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `check_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `is_enabled` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  `schedule_cron` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `threshold_value` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_run_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_status` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_output` text,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`server_check_id`),\n  KEY `server_checks_ibfk_1` (`server_id`),\n  KEY `server_checks_ibfk_2` (`check_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `server_checks_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`server_id`) REFERENCES `servers` (`server_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `server_checks_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`check_id`) REFERENCES `checks` (`check_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `check_results` (\n  `result_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `server_check_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `run_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `output` text,\n  `duration_ms` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `threshold_breached` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`result_id`),\n  KEY `check_results_ibfk_1` (`server_check_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `check_results_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`server_check_id`) REFERENCES `server_checks` (`server_check_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `alerts` (\n  `alert_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `result_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `alert_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `message` varchar(1000) NOT NULL,\n  `severity` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `is_resolved` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `sent_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `resolved_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`alert_id`),\n  KEY `alerts_ibfk_1` (`result_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `alerts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`result_id`) REFERENCES `check_results` (`result_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
